From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 23:29:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 167/184] Sega CD: letter all four devices, and stop formatting
 the backup RAM

A drive with no filesystem on it got no drive letter, so there was no
way to reach it from the desktop and no way to hand it to FORMATI.PRG --
which is why the internal backup RAM was formatted during boot: that was
the only moment it could ever be done. The disc and the cartridge
escaped only because internal_inquire() calls them removable and
disk_init_one() force-adds a partition to a removable unit whatever the
root sector says.

disk_init_one() now force-adds one for any SEGACD_BUS device that got
nothing from atari_partition(). The four devices always exist; whether
there is a filesystem on them is the owner's business. An unformatted
drive still fails to open, which is honest.

So bram_probe() no longer formats. Once both drives worked, formatting
whatever did not look like ours on every boot was a standing offer to
delete somebody's game saves. Holding C at power-on still reformats,
which is the recovery path for a damaged volume of ours, and the
signature repair on a volume that is already ours is unchanged.
---
 bios/disk.c   | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 bios/segacd.c | 23 ++++++++++-------------
 2 files changed, 29 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/disk.c b/bios/disk.c
index b3e6049..05005b2 100644
--- a/bios/disk.c
+++ b/bios/disk.c
@@ -266,6 +266,25 @@ static void disk_init_one(UWORD unit,LONG *devices_available)
     atari_partition(unit,devices_available);
     devs ^= *devices_available; /* which ones were allocated this time */
 
+#ifdef MACHINE_SEGACD
+    /* The Mega CD's four devices always exist, formatted or not.
+     *
+     * Without this a drive with no filesystem on it gets no letter, so
+     * there is no way to reach it from the desktop and no way to format
+     * it -- which is how the internal backup RAM came to be formatted
+     * during boot instead: it was the only moment it could be done. The
+     * disc and the cartridge escaped that only because
+     * internal_inquire() calls them removable and the block below
+     * force-adds a partition to a removable unit whatever the root
+     * sector says. That is the right behaviour and it should not depend
+     * on lying about whether a chip is soldered down.
+     *
+     * An unformatted drive still fails to open. That is honest, and
+     * FORMATS.PRG and FORMATI.PRG are there for it. */
+    if (GET_BUS(unit - NUMFLOPPIES) == SEGACD_BUS && !devs)
+        add_partition(unit,devices_available,"GEM",0L,0L);
+#endif
+
     /*
      * now ensure that we have a minimum number of logical devices
      * for a removable physical unit
diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index e9f17ac..5586142 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -4413,21 +4413,18 @@ static void bram_probe(void)
         return;
     }
 
-    /* Anything else gets formatted.
+    /* Nothing else happens. It is not formatted, it is not touched.
      *
-     * There was a guard here: find Sega's "SEGA_CD_ROM" footer, assume
-     * game saves, refuse to touch it. It fired on every console ever
-     * made -- the machine's own BIOS formats this memory before the
-     * owner sees it -- so I: never worked anywhere, while the desktop
-     * carried an icon for it that errored when clicked. Narrowing it to
-     * "formatted and not empty" would have been a better guard and
-     * still the wrong idea: this is eight kilobytes on a console that
-     * somebody has deliberately booted a 1985 desktop operating system
-     * on, from a disc they burned to do it. They know what it is for.
+     * It used to be formatted right here, because a drive with no
+     * filesystem got no drive letter and boot was the only moment it
+     * could ever be done. disk.c now gives the Mega CD's four devices a
+     * letter whether or not there is a filesystem on them, so an
+     * unformatted volume can be reached from the desktop and handed to
+     * FORMATI.PRG -- and formatting on every boot, once both drives
+     * worked, was a standing offer to delete somebody's files.
      *
-     * If those saves matter, SRAMTOOL.PRG copies the memory off before
-     * this ever runs. */
-    bram_format();
+     * Holding C at power-on still reformats, which is the recovery path
+     * for a volume of ours that has been damaged. */
     bram_state = BRAM_OURS;
 }
 
